1 Introduction
The food production targets must be increased by 60–100% for feeding an exponentially increasing 9.7 billion global population by 2050 [1, 2]. Therefore, intensive agriculture productive agrosystems coupled with novel agri-technologies are adopted worldwide in achieving food security goals. However, the limited fertile land and accessible natural resources, soil degradation, depletion of soil organic matter (SOM), nutrients imbalance in soil and drastic climate changes are the major constraints for crop production and food security worldwide [3, 4]. In addition, injudicious use of agrochemicals (mainly chemical fertilizers and pesticides) and plant growth enhancers in these intensive agriculture practices has resulted in environmental pollution and community health threats [5, 6]. Therefore, there is an exigent need for climateadapted sustainable agricultural practices for sustained food production from cultivable land. With significant increases in crop yields by using novel agri-technologies recently, large quantities of agricultural wastes or organic wastes (OWs) are also produced globally [7, 8]. Organic wastes contain materials, which have originated from living organisms and are biodegradable. Organic wastes comprises of agricultural waste, municipal solid waste, industrial solid waste and wastewaters [9, 10]. On their source of origin, agricultural wastes are categorised into four major types, which includes livestock waste, agricultural waste, agroindustrial residues and aquaculture wastes (Fig. 1). Around 120 million tons of waste is produced annually from livestock sector, which generally refers to cattle, poultry and pig farm waste including urine, dung, wastewater, waste feed, feather, hoof, horn, bedding material etc., and waste from slaughter houses [11]. In addition, about 200 billion tons of crop plant residues and plant biomass is formed annually in the world, nearly 90% of which is lignocellulosic [10, 12]. Post-harvest agricultural waste is considered as primary agricultural waste and it includes leaf litter, straw, husk, weeds, seed pods and plant stalks. Cellulose that accounts for 40–50% of the plant dry weight is the main constituent of lignicellulosic waste, and is rich in organic carbon and other plant nutrients. Agro-industrial residues are considered as secondary agricultural wastes as these are the industrial by-products of primary agricultural residues and waste products from food processing units. They generally include bagasse, molasses, pulp and peels of different fruits and vegetables, oil seed cakes etc. In recent years, aquaculture waste is also considered as a type of agricultural wastes containing aquaculture residues such as uneaten feed and their fecal waste [8, 10]. Accumulation of vast amount of agricultural wastes in soil along with the build-up of toxic compounds, heavy metals (HMs), pesticides, salts, and pathogens causes soil pollution that adversely affect health of plants, animals and humans, and also cause major economic losses [6, 13, 14]. Contaminated/polluted soil is not ideal for agricultural, industrial or other recreational activities as it reduces soil fertility and negatively impact crop productivity [15, 16]. Heavy metals like lead, arsenic, mercury, nickel, zinc and copper are also being introduced in the soil through different agricultural activities such as long-term application of various agrochemicals and urban sewage sludge in crop fields, waste incineration
