and release of untreated waste in the farm and environment [17, 18]. All these HM pollutants are potential health risks to microbes, plants, animals and humans, because of their transfer from soil to plants [19–21]. Farmers generally leave agricultural waste in the fields or burn them. Burning of agricultural residues releases 141.15 Mt of CO2, 8.77 Mt of CO, 0.12 Mt of NH3 and 0.23 Mt of NO [22] and it contributes to global warming. This also causes soil contamination and loss of soil organic matter (SOM) content to the tune of 80–90% N, 25% P and 20% K [12, 23]. Thus, depletion of SOM, HM pollutants, salinization and nutrient imbalance in soil affect soil microbial population and their beneficial activities, nutrient cycling and physical characteristics of soil [4, 24] and many agro-practices have been proposed to improve the level of SOM for ensuring sustainable fertility and crop productivity of degraded soil. Conventional methods of solid waste management include thermal processes (incineration, gasification or uncontrolled pyrolysis), chemical degradation and landfilling. These high cost and energy consuming processes negatively impact the environment by discharging a variety of harmful chemicals and greenhouse gases. These problems have necessitated development of sustainable, economical and socially acceptable organic waste management techniques for management of solid organic waste, which may help in increasing the economic value of the discarded organic wastes [25]. Therefore, biological conversion of agricultural waste has emerged as a sustainable and economic option, and addition of processed OWs to soil enhances the nutrient content and fertility of soil [26–28]. Several technologies have been developed for biodegradation of OWs such as composting, vermi-composting, anaerobic digestion, production of biochar and organic phytostimulants etc. [29–31]. These biological technologies utilize different indigenous and exogenous organisms such as bacteria, fungi, algae, protozoa, nematodes and arthropods to decompose and process agricultural waste [32–34]. For instance, olive mill wastewater sludge generated by the olive oil industry was converted into highly beneficial organo-mineral fertilizers after processing [35, 36] and its addition improved soil properties, including water availability, and the nutrient concentrations, such as P, K, and magnesium (Mg). Furthermore, addition of organo-mineral fertilizer (OMF) formulations on Zea mays positively affected the plant’s respiration, photosynthetic activity, and nutrient assimilations [37]. Organic wastes can also be used for energy recovery (such as heat, liquid fuels, biohydrogen, or electricity), as well as for the production of certain chemicals, such as volatile organic acids and alcohols [38–40]. Microbial decomposition of OWs under aerobic and anaerobic conditions is the most efficient method of decomposition, and single or co-application of organic amendments such as compost and biochar has been reported to stimulate microbial community and improved the fertility of deteriorated soil with low organic matter content [41–43]. Also, many studies have focused on the development of agricultural waste based biofertilizers to replenish macro- and micro-nutrients in polluted soils and to minimize the use of agrochemicals in farmlands [33, 44]. Another sustainable approach to mitigate soil pollution is bioremediation, which is used for removal or stabilization of HMs and decontamination of pesticides in polluted soil [21, 45, 46]. Bouhia et al. [32] identified potent native fungal species, FUN 06 (Galactomyces geotrichum), within olive mill wastewater sludge (OMWS) suitable for bioremediation and its transformation into a high-value organic fertilizer using a mixture of OMWS and rock phosphate (RP). While there have been numerous research papers and reviews that discuss the approaches used for organic waste management [26, 30–32]. However, there has been little comprehensive discussion about the potential contribution of processed OWs and associated beneficial microbes in different biological processes, pollution mitigation and crop production (although see, 25, 37, 47). In the present manuscript, a broad overview has been provided about the main strategies employed for management and processing of different kinds of OWs in improving nutrition biavailability, functioning of various biological processes, bioremediation of soil pollutants and for increasing crop productivity in a sustainable way. A more in-depth discussion about novel approaches and strategies has been provided about various mechanisms of OWs processing and microbial communities for increasing their efficacy to use processed OWs as organic fertilizers for environmental sustainability.
2 Need for soil fertilization with exogenous organic matter
Earth’s soil resources provide nutrients for growth of plants and soil-inhabiting microorganisms. Due to recent intensive agricultural practices and soil erosion, agricultural soils are degrading faster with reduction in organic matter than it is naturally replenished. In addition, the uncontrolled use of agrochemicals and mounting apprehensions over contamination of the environment has increased the need for safe and nutritious food. Therefore, future of humanity over the next century is severely threatened by unsustainable soil management practices [48]. Thus, organic farming has gained significant global attention recently to maximize the agronomic efficiency and crop productivity [49]. Organic farming practice employs processed organic waste products for achieving optimal agricultural productivity with conservation of


