agroecosystems and proper functioning of soil ecosystems, while minimizing adverse impacts on both the environment and human communities on soil health and fertility [50, 51]. Soil-inhabiting microorganisms play important role in nutrients’ solubilization and bioavailabity for plants, formation of soil organic carbon (SOC), its preservation and loss [52, 53]. Soil organic carbon in living soil is a major engine of terrestrial biogeochemistry, which regulates the turnover of terrestrial C pool and soil organic matter, act as a source of plant nutrients for ecosystem productivity, provide diverse energy sources for soil microorganisms to express beneficial activities, act as fundamental control on soil structure, and plays a critical role in terrestrial ecosystem functioning [54–56]. Unfortunately, the level of SOC is quite low in most upland soils and it ranges from 0.5% to 3.0% organic C in the topsoil. Therefore, soil fertilization with exogenous organic matter (EOM), including composts, vermicomposts or biochar of organic waste origin prepared from agriculture, forestry or industry, is considered essential in improving the fertility and organic matter content of soil for sustainable development and circular economy around the world [57]. Soil organic matter significantly improves the soil’s capacity to store and supply essential nutrients (such as N, P, K, Ca and Mg), and to retain toxic elements. It allows the soil to cope with changes in soil acidity, and helps soil minerals to decompose faster. Ayangbenro et al. [58] reported that understanding the continuing transformations of organic amendments and the distribution of different factors (carbon, nutrients, and microbial activities) across and along roots is crucial in the soil and rhizosphere for promoting plant growth and health. Liu et al. [59] showed that organic manure fertilization in wheat greatly decreased the network complexity ratio of the rhizosphere to bulk soil for bacteria and fungi. The proportion of bacteria in the rhizosphere derived from the bulk soil was much higher in comparison to fungi. In another study, addition of exogenous straw as green management practice in maize rhizosphere accelerated nutrient cycling by triggering microbial growth and biomass, and synthesizing a large number of enzymes including β-1,4glucosidase (BG) and β-N-acetylglucosaminidase (NAG) hydrolases [60]. In contrast, the addition of biochar increased the relative abundance of Actinobacteria, enhanced the root biomass by 31%, increased mobilization of nutrients in the rhizosphere, and expression of BG and NAG was accelerated by 26 and 47%, respectively by expanding the extent of rhizosphere hotspots from a larger soil volume. Earlier studies showed that addition of SOM regulated soil phosphorus (P) dynamics and producing phytoavailable P in soils for effective management of soil health and enhancement of soil fertility, especially P-use efficiency [61]. Massaccesi et al. [62] observed stimulation of the microbial community and increases in the enzymatic activities in compost (CMP) amended soils, indicating that the organic matter provided by the compost is more readily mineralized than that added with the biochar. Moreover, the biochar (BIO) amended soils showed the lowest qCO2 and ecoenzymatic C:N ratio, and the highest qMIC (1.4%), indicating a C immobilization process into the microbial biomass, which need increases in N-cycling enzyme activity. In the short term, a boost of soil C and N cycling was evident with CMP and BIO amendments, respectively. In another experiment, Yang et al. [63] demonstrated that incorporation of organic amendment (Grass peat) improved soil nutrients, organic C sequestration, water use efficiency (WUE) and yield of maize (Zea mays L.) under dryland agriculture in China. In a recent study, two green manure treatments [Vicia villosa (hairy vetch) and Brassica juncea (L.)] were applied with and without Trichoderma spp. strain RW309, and control (soil only) [64]. Plant growth was significantly higher with green manure combined with RW309 inoculation than with green manure alone. The enzyme activities and fungal biomass were also markedly higher in the co-application treatments than in the other treatments. Moreover, co-incorporation of green manure and RW309 enhanced specific fungal genera Penicillium spp., which further stimulated mineralization of nutrients, enhanced plant growth, and improved soil health for sustainable agricultural practices.
3 Effect of agricultural wastes and pollutants on physiology and functioning of crop plants
Soil is the fundamental requirement for sustaining life on earth [53] and it is constantly changing and developing with time in response to environmental factors. Some of these changes are short-termed and reversible, while some become permanent characteristics of the soil. Increased agricultural production required for exponentaially growing population has led to large accumulation of agricultural wastes and their by-products, and causing soil pollution and soil degradation [65, 66]. In addition, litter quality has also been demonstrated to control the contribution of microbial carbon to main microbial groups and SOC during its decomposition [67]. Moreover, the burden of climate change mediated environmental stresses can be easily seen on the environment especially on soil [68, 69]. Furthermore, the buildup of


