toxic pollutants in soil negatively affects the physico-chemical and biological features of soil and adversely affects the growth and development of plants, resulting in lowering the crop yield [14, 65, 70, 71]. Different pollutants including organic waste, industrial waste (such as molasses, bagasse, and processed food waste), agrochemicals (such as pesticides, insecticides, herbicides and chemical fertilizers) and HMs (mainly lead mercury, cyanides, copper, zinc, cadmium etc.) contribute towards soil pollution [17, 65]. Many of the pesticides and herbicides are still used for agricultural purposes in developing countries to control the pests for increasing the crop yield, but their injudicious use alters the biodiversity of the soil [71]. These pesticides include highly persistent and toxic chemicals such as aldrin, chlordane, DDT, dieldrin, endrin, heptachlor, mirex, polychlorinated biphenyls, polychlorinated dibenzo-p-dioxins, polychlorinated dibenzofurans and toxaphen [72, 73]. Industrial products such as detergents, petroleum hydrocarbons, plastics, organic solvents, pesticides, and dyes, are the vital sources of organic pollutants and are the additional sources of soil pollution. Some other organic pollutants in wastewater include phenols, oil, surfactants, pesticides, herbicides, excreta of animals etc. [32, 74]. These organic pollutants are very slowly degraded by microflora inhabiting soil and water; hence, they keep on accumulating in the soil. As a result, they negatively affect the plant growth by inhibiting their overall growth, while reducing the yield and size of the grain and fruit. Presence of HMs in soil at low concentrations have little effect the plant growth, but after a threshold limit, the plants will be poisoned by these HMs leading to their death [18, 75, 76]. In a study by Sandalio et al. [77] observed reduced plant growth in soils contaminated with high levels of cadmium (Cd) due to decreased nutrient uptake from contaminated soil, inhibition of various enzyme activities and initiation of oxidative stress in plants. Bini et al. [78] reported negative effects on root and shoot fresh weight by application of wastewater, which was polluted with HMs and other organic pollutants. The polluted wastewater caused reduction in water and nutrient uptake by roots leading to decreased relative water content and subsequently damaged the roots leading to retardation effects on plant growth. Generally, plants absorb the nutrients and water along with pollutants and/or their degradation products from soil, and from plants, these pollutants enter food chains; hence affecting health of animals and humans [6]. On the other hand, fertile and healthy soils provide sufficient nutrients for crop plants resulting in increased nutrient-rich crop yields, whereas contaminated soil leads to poor crop yield with decreased nutritional value. Ahmed et al. [79] reported that irrigation of turnip and lettuce plants with industrial waste water negatively influenced all the measured yield components like number of pods, fresh weight per plant, seed number and weight per plant, and seed index. In some cases, livestock and human excreta is also used directly as organic manure to enhance crop production but its addition may result in soil pollution and its contamination with various pathogens, which may be present in the excreta. The number of beneficial bacteria, fungi and earthworms present in soil that bind it together start to decline, changing the soil structure and creating an additional problem of soil erosion.
4 Sustainable strategies for management of organic waste and pollution mitigation in soil
Due to mounting apprehensions over contamination of the environment resulting from the uncontrolled use of agrochemicals and increasing need for safe and nutritious food, organic farming has gained significant global attention recently [49]. Organic farming practice employs processed OW products (including composting, vermi-compost, biogas slurry and biochar) for achieving optimal agricultural productivity with conservation of agroecosystems and proper functioning of soil ecosystems, while minimizing adverse impacts on both the environment and human communities [50]. Therefore, OW management has emerged as an issue of concern for researchers and environmentalists involved in ecological and sustainable agriculture all over the world [80, 81]. In comparison to conventional approach using unprocessed or untreated OW, a good organic waste utilization technology rapidly converts the organic waste residues into readily available, nutrient rich material without producing any harmful and unsuitable end products [32, 82, 83]. This necessitates the use of better technology and incentives, and a better approach to organic waste management. According to USDA agricultural waste management field handbook, an agricultural waste management system is a well-planned system designed to regulate and use agricultural by-products in such a way that maintains or upgrades the quality of the environment (air, water, soil, plant, and animal resources) [84, 85]. Such systems are designed and planned to manage all types of agricultural waste for utilization throughout the year. An organic waste management system is a function of six fundamental steps, including waste production, collection, storage, treatment, utilization and transfer as well as supervision of waste material throughout the process [86]. It starts with the production and accumulation of different waste and residues followed by their collection from original production source or from dumping site. Next, the waste is temporarily stored in different storage facilities until its treatment.


