Different activities like analyzing the features of the waste, figuring out the preferred features of the waste after treatment, selecting the kind of treatment facility, as well as its projected size, location, and installation costs, and calculating the total cost of the treatment process must be considered for waste treatment. After appropriate treatment and processing of waste material, OWs are utilized as organic fertilizers for enhancing crop productivity along with mitigation of soil pollution. Another important step is the transfer of waste material throughout the process i.e., from source to collection site, to storage facility, to treatment plants and at last to the site of implication [85]. It also involves the supervision and after-care of disposal sites so that the waste and/or pathogens from the waste do not migrate into the population. Till now, most of the research to utilize OWs has focused on conversion of these wastes for fertilizers, bio-energy and biogas production [25, 87–89]. For instance, Babu et al. [39] revealed that biomass from agricultural waste may be used to create second-generation biofuels and may serve as a fodder for animals and raw material industrial products. In organic production systems, composted agricultural waste can replace energy-intensive chemical fertilizers [31, 90]. Thus, development of environmentally robust agri-waste residue/OW management technologies has the potential to mitigate soil pollution in situ and ex situ, and contribute towards establishing a green economy in the area of agricultural production. Composting, vermi-composting and biochar production are some of the predominant methods used in recycling of organic wastes, which are subsequently used in sustainable agriculture for improving crop production [91, 92]. Various biological processes used in OW management are briefly discussed in this section.
4.1 Production of compost from organic wastes
Composting process provides the most efficient way of handling agricultural OWs and it has emerged as a basic and valuable technique for preparing a nutrient rich fertilizer from OW materials [26, 37, 93]. Thus, OWs may be effectively recycled in the system itself as organic manure that acts as a soil conditioner in agricultural soils improving soil’s fertility, structure, water holding capacity and buffering capacity [94]. Therefore, the process of composting is advantageous for disposing OW materials from an ecological and financial standpoint. Composting involves the aerobic/anaerobic and mesophilic/thermophilic breakdown of putrescible in organic waste by microorganisms and certain invertebrates [9, 95]. This process has also proven to enhance pesticide degradation in soil as well as in compost heaps [96, 97]. The final product of composting is a stable organic material, which is particularly relevant to agricultural production and management of forest lands. Application of compost in soil has also helped in reducing loss of N from soil as compared to application of fresh manure. Recently, Hang et al. [93] showed that fertilizers produced through composting process contain higher organic matter, averaging 45.42%, compared to commercial organic fertilizers and bio-organic fertilizers. It was predicted that organic matter content could be increased to 49.3% by optimization of equipment and composting processes in Chinese organic fertilizer companies, which will potentially reduce annual carbon emissions by an estimated 3.07 to 6.97 billion kg of CO2 equivalent. Based on the raw material used for the process, compost can be classified as green compost and brown compost. Green compost is prepared from plant byproducts such as crop residues, leaf and litter waste and yard wastes. Brown compost is prepared mainly from wet wastes such as animal manure, kitchen wastes and municipal organic waste [98]. Compost can also be classified as aerobic composting and anaerobic composting depending on the process conditions. For instance, Foughal et al. [89] inoculated a mixture of agri-food waste including fish, vegetable, and poultry wastes with Aspergillus niger for accelerating waste biotransformation in a 20-day process. A combination of all three waste types with a tiny excess of poultry waste was found as the best, effective and secure mixture for biofertilization. Anaerobic digestion product had a higher potential for biofertilization than aerobic biotransformation product, according to fertilization tests on tomato crops.
4.1.1 Aerobic composting
Composting done under aerobic conditions is the most popular and efficient technique of converting OWs into compost (Fig. 2), which produces mature compost within a short time period [99]. During the process, the raw waste material is biologically converted to a stable, pathogen-free and odour less product ready to apply in agriculture and forestry [100, 101]. The product can be obtained within around 4 weeks with regular turning of compost pile [91]. However, if the compost is not turned regularly, it may affect the quality and duration of compost. Sufficient amount of oxygen and aerated conditions are required during the aerobic composting process. A compost pile containing organic waste, sufficient amount of moisture with balanced carbon and nitrogen ratio (C:N) is set up in pits or in an open area. Mesophilic microorganisms (ideal temperature ranges from 20 to 45 °C) start to multiply in the


