pile metabolizing complex polysaccharides, while producing heat [26, 102]. With increase in the temperature of the compost pile, it becomes ideal for growth and functioning of thermophilic microorganisms (ideal temperature 50 to 70 °C) [102]. Further increases in the pile temperature (to > 62 °C), destroys the pathogens and weed seeds present in the compost, hence improving compost quality [103]. After decomposition of major OWs, the pile temperature reduces gradually, allowing the mesophiles to take over the process once again [104]. The last step in this process is curing. Curing phase occurs when the pile stops reheating even after turning the pile. This step indicates completion of the composting process. The final product is a dark-brown or black with reduced particle size and it lacks active microorganisms. Mineralization and humification are the main steps of degrading organic matter through composting [103]. The time duration of compost formation varies with different technologies. Most popular composting methods include Indian Bangalore composting, windrow composting, static composting, sheet composting, Indian Indore composting and Berkley rapid composting [9]. The efficiency of different technologies for composting OWs depends on the raw materials used, microbial inoculum, site of composting, climatic conditions, moisture content, aeration conditions and dimensions of the heap [102, 103]. The current knowledge on improving the efficacy of composting due to inoculation of efficient microorganisms in a compost pile containing OWs, and the avenues for future research, leading to a holistic view and understanding of nutrient-rich compost are discussed in this section. Strong microbial activity during composting improves the quality of the nutrients in the compost by the activity of nitrifiers, ammonia oxidizers, sulphur oxidizers, and nitrogen-fixing microorganisms [104, 105]. For instance, inoculation of sugarcane trash and rice straw with cellulolytic microorganisms such as Aspergillus awamori, Trichoderma viride, Phanerochaete chrysosporium, Trichorus spiralis and Paecilomyces fusisporus enhanced the composting processs and produced composts with lower C:N ratio. In addition, inoculation with N2-fixing Azotobacter, phosphate-solubilizing fungus Aspergillus awamori and cellulolytic fungi along with rock phosphate (RP) helped to produce composts from plant residues in shorter period and with highest N and P content. Bouhia et al. [106] showed that Pichia was the most represented genus with almost 53% of the total identified fungal population in raw olive mill wastewater sludge composting. Moreover, the bacteria that dominated were Zymobacter
