ed both shoot as well as belowground root components, and significantly affect bacterial diversity than fungal diversity as compared to control. Proteobacteria, Firmicutes, Bacteroidetes, Acidobacteria, and Cyanobacteria responded positively to fertilizers, while only Ascomycota and Basidiomycota were influenced among fungal phyla. Redundancy analysis indicated shifts in community structure linked to soil chemical properties. In another recent study, effects of microbial agents were evaluated on N conversion during cotton straw composting [113]. Microbial inoculation enhanced the abundance of nifH, glnA, and amoA genes and reduced the major denitrification genes (nirK, narG, and nirS). Network analysis model showed distinct changing patterns of bacterial communities after inoculation, and identified keystone microorganisms in maintaining network complexity and system function to promote N preservation. Microbial inoculation also increased the germination index and total nitrogen by 24–29% and 7–10 g kg−1, respectively.
4.1.2 Anaerobic composting
Anaerobic composting is carried out in the absence of oxygen in an enclosed system, commonly known as digesters, by utilizing anaerobic microorganisms. During this process, a mixture of different gases (primarily methane and carbon dioxide, with traces of hydrogen sulfide and hydrogen), collectively known as biogas, is produced [94]. The methane content determines biogas’ calorific value, while CO2 acts as a contaminant. The spent slurry or compost can be used as manure in agriculture and forestry [114]. During anaerobic composting, superior compost and liquid fertilizer per unit volume of raw material is produced as there is minimal nutrient loss as compared to aerobic digestion. However,


