se of chemical fertilizers and environmental problems globally. Deng et al. [119] used an amino acid-derived ionic liquid, glycine hydrochloride, to enhance methane production from anaerobic digestion of rice straw. The addition of glycine hydrochloride increased bacterial diversity and increased the abundance of methanogens and archaea. The peak activities of cellulase, xylanase, and lignin peroxidase enzymes were significantly higher than those of the control (without additive). The cumulative methane production was 115.56 mL g−1 with glycine hydrochloride additive, which was 73% higher than that of the control after 40 days of digestion. Czekała et al. [120] reviewed the effect of reutilizing anaerobic digestate solid fraction on the composting process. The solid digestate functioned as an important substrate for the manufacture of compost, both as a monosubstrate and as a desirable supplement for additional raw materials to supplement organic matter to the soil. Thus, anaerobic digestate solid fraction was established as innovative process-improving additive for improving composting process, and may be applied in land reclamation. Moreover, its implementation in modern bioeconomy will provide effective waste management. Machine learning (ML) technique was used to predict specific methane yields (SMY) with a dataset of 14 features from lignocellulosic biomass (LB) characteristics and operating conditions of completely mixed reactors under continuous feeding mode [121]. Biomass compositions greatly influenced SMYs from LB, and cellulose prevailed over lignin and biomass ratio as the most important feature. Impact of LB to manure ratio was assessed to optimize biogas production with the random forest (RF) model. Under typical organic loading rates (OLR), optimum LB to manure ratio of 1:1 was identified. Experimental results confirmed successful applications of ML for anaerobic digestion modelling and influential factors were revealed by the RF model that provided the highest SMY of 79.2% of the predicted value. In another study, hydrochar co-compost (HCO) was formed by anaerobic breakdown of municipal solid waste (MSW) combined with hydrothermal carbonization (HTC) [122]. HCO may subsequently be used to recover biogas and compost. Interestingly, hydrochar co-compost cytotoxicity was found similar to standard compost cytotoxicity. Wang et al. [123] conducted their research on the metabolic features of bacterial and fungal groups at the time of composting as well as the use of biochar and biogas leftovers in composting. It was established that about 50% the functional genes involved in carbon fixation, organic matter decomposition and methane oxidation were found of acsA (gene encoding acetyl-CoA synthetase) abundance. On the other hand, abundance of denitrification gene nirK (encoding nitrite reductase enzyme; is involved in denitrification converting nitrite to nitric oxide) was dramatically reduced by the presence of biogas residue in the compost. Biogas residues were also found to enhance population of bacterial as well as fungal communities. The redundancy analysis showed that pile temperature, pH and EC were the key physico-chemical parameters influencing the expression of microbial community nirK gene.
4.1.3 Production of humic substances
Humic substances (HSs) occupy 80% of organic matter in soil and significantly influence soil physico-chemical properties, and have been widely applied for soil remediation agents, and adsorbents [124]. The biochemical reactions involved in aerobic composting degrades organic matter and the products aggregates to produce ‘humic like substances’ [91, 125]. HSs are a core product of pre-controlled degradation and chemical oxidation of OWs, and a crucial indicator of


