the maturation of compost that can be used in soil amendments. These HSs can be obtained from naturally humified organic matter, from composts or vermi-composts or from mineral deposits [126]. Humic substances as well as their compounds in soil (humins, humic acid and fulvic acid) are a result of interaction between organic matter, microbes and plant roots [127, 128]. They can also be formed ex situ by controlled breakdown and oxidation of OWs resulting in humic like substances. Since the HS extraction rate is very low by microbial degradation in nature, artificial humification processes such as aerobic composting and hydrothermal treatment have attracted a great deal of attention as the most important strategies in HS production [124]. The formation of HSs is complex process and is affected by the characteristics of the raw materials involved, the presence of compost additives, microbial activity, temperature, pH, the C/N ratio, moisture content, oxygen content and particle size, all of which can interact with each other. Humic substances can be divided into humic acids (HAs), fulvic acids (FAs), and humins based on their characteristics in alkaline and acidic solutions. However, HSs represent the same functional groups and structural arrangements, which help to predict their structures. Functional groups represented by phenol and carboxylic acid groups of HAs and FAs can provide various agronomic functions, such as plant growth enhancement, water and nutrient retention, and disease suppression capacity. Thus, HSs can act as a soil amendment, fertilizer, and plant growth regulator. The effect of HSs varies with soil environment, the nutrient accepting plant, the dosage and method of application. Humic substances strongly affect solubility of several plant nutrients such as P, Fe, and Cu, thereby increasing their plant availability [129]. In addition, SOC, mainly HSs, can detoxify monomeric Al in acid soils, can strongly bind toxic heavy metals, making them unavailable to the plant roots, and may strongly bind a vast variety of harmful organic pollutants [124, 130]. The inclusion of farmyard manure (FYM) and its rotted or composted forms were found superior compared to the separate application of straw and slurry to soil. Soils amended with polyanionic HSs shows enhanced cation exchange capacity leading to increased nutrient uptake. Humic substances also stimulate H+-ATPase activity for uptake of nitrate and other nutrients. They also help in stress mitigation by phenyl-propanoid metabolism leading to the production of phenolic compounds [91, 124]. These functions of HSs enhance the reuse potential of organic waste compost products; however, this requires scientific control of various composting parameters and appropriate application of final products.
4.2 Decomposition of organic waste material into vermicompost
Vermicomposting is a type of aerobic composting based on the decaying of organic biomass with the help of earthworms and some associated microorganisms. This is a mesophilic process, where the end product i.e., vermicompost is a stable, humus-like material [131, 132]. The species that are used for vermicomposting are usually epigeic [133], and they live in areas containing high amount of organic matter feeding on leaf litter, decaying plant roots or dung. Species of earthworms used in this process remain on the soil surface and do not form permanent burrows. In this process, different earthworm species most commonly used includes Eudrilus eugeniae, Perionyx excavatus, Eisenia andrei, E. fetida [132, 134], and Pontoscolex corethrurus [135].
With time, many variations in vermicomposting methodology have been developed. It may vary from composting in windrows to completely automated, continuous-flow reactor arrangements [136]. The process of vermicomposting occurs in two phases: (i) the active decomposition phase where the earthworms ingest organic waste, digest it and excrete simplified organic matter. They also assist the movement and involvement of poorly mobile microbial communities. Earthworms also fractionate the organic matter, thus allowing an increased surface area exposure to other decomposer microbes [137], and during the maturation stage, earthworms travel to freshly added undigested organic waste. This step is necessary to collect earthworm-free vermicompost while not disturbing the process. During the organic matter decomposition by earthworms, it has been demonstrated that pathogen removal occurs while transportation through the gastrointestinal track of the worm [138] and also during the process of composting. However, for complete pathogen removal and increased decomposition rate, combining of vermicomposting and composting is preferred. Similar to composting process, vermicomposting also produces greenhouse gases like CO2 and N2O depending on the raw material used [139]. The rate of decomposition, final product quality, worm mortality and greenhouse gas emission all depends on the vermicomposting parameters and therefore, the process parameters must be kept under control. Zhou et al. [140] provided an overview for future waste management developments using various microbial cultures either individually or in the form of consortium, supplements, bulking agents, or their amalgamations for process improvement. Recent advancements in combining mathematical modelling, innovative supplements, and intelligenceassisted device designs with (vermi-) composting practices were highlighted. Maharjan et al. [141] assessed the suitability of vermicomposting for various types of OWs based on earthworm growth and reproduction as well as nutrient


