4.3 Production of biochar from organic waste
Biochar is a compact solid attained after the thermo-chemical alteration of organic waste material in anaerobic environment [40, 143]. The Amazonian "Terra Preta" soils were first discovered in the nineteenth century by European explorers in the Amazonian region that contain large amounts of biochar derived carbon with greater microbial activity, better nutrient availability and crop production during exploration [82, 144, 145]. Recently, biochar has appeared as a potential supplement to soils as well as composts with many positive outcomes [42, 146, 147]. Generally, the carbon stored by plants via photosynthesis is returned to the atmosphere during natural decomposition of dead plants, thus completing the carbon cycle [25]. However, converting dead biomass into biochar disturbs the carbon cycle by storing carbon in stable form [40, 148]. Biochar is produced from various waste biomass sources, such as wood, manure, wheat or paddy straw, sewage sludge and municipal refuse [41, 149]. Biochar is produced by the method of pyrolysis under controlled conditions. The pyrolysis reactor’s temperature typically varies from 300 to 1000 °C. Pyrolysis of plant biomass results in three streams of products: (i) noncondensable gases, (ii) a combustible bio-oil (liquid hydrocarbon) and water, (iii) biochar (solid byproduct) [150, 151]. The pyrolysis reaction can be either exothermic or endothermic based on the temperature of the reactants, but with decrease in reaction temperature, it becomes increasingly exothermic [152]. Also, the method should be energy-efficient and pyrolytic gases should be utilized within the procedure to reduce emissions [153]. Compared to pyrolysis at higher temperatures, instead of; pyrolysis at lower temperatures often results in a higher biochar yield and an improved volatile matter composition [154]. There are estimates of exothermicity or energy yield of 2.0 to 3.2 kJ g−1 of biochar on slow pyrolysis [152]. Among different methods used for pyrolysis, microwave assisted pyrolysis is carried out at 300–500 °C ranging from minutes to hours, whereas slow pyrolysis is performed at 300–700 °C, and it takes hours [151]. At temperature range of 450–550 °C, pyrolysis hardly takes 1 min (fast pyrolysis) and flash pyrolysis is carried out 300–800 °C temperature within 1 s. Other pyrolysis methods include torrefaction (200–320 °C for hours), hydrothermal carbonisation (150–400 °C for minutes to hours) and gasification (> 800 °C for seconds to minutes). This allows regulation of physico-chemical properties of biochar. Production of biochar also requires careful selection of feedstock and optimization of processing conditions to achieve maximum possible stability. Zhu et al. [155] stated that one of the most popular management techniques for achieving carbon neutrality in a circular economy is the pyrolysis of agro-residues into biochar. Agro-residues may be pyrolyzed to make biochar, which can then be used as an alternative energy source, a soil conditioner, and a replacement for activated carbon to create biochar that is more environmentally friendly and long-lasting. Krishnamoorthy et al. [156] reported that biochar properties were significantly affected by feedstock type and pyrolysis temperature. Sawdust biochar (lignocellulosic biomass) produced at 5000C had the highest struvite yield from urine (7.91 g L−1), phosphate (97.9%) and ammonium recovery (87.1%), and relative crystal size (85.2%) compared to the non-seeded treatment due to its higher surface area, pore volume, and hydrophobicity of the biochar. Manure pellet biochar (non-lignocellulosic biomass) produced at 5000C also exhibited performance comparable to sawdust biochar produced at 5000C. Increasing pyrolysis temperature increased biochar’s hydrophobicity, zeta potential, electrophoretic mobility and bulk density, irrespective of the feedstock type, thereby improving the seeding process. The ash content of biochar was negatively correlated with its surface area, pore volume, and particle size, but positively correlated with biochar’s bulk density and suspension stability. In another study, Sun et al. [92] systematically investigated the effect of pyrolysis temperature on the structure of biochar prepared from crop straw of three crops (cotton, alfalfa, and wheat). A higher pyrolysis temperature led to a higher aromatization and graphitization degree of biochar and a numerical relationship between pyrolysis temperature and biochar surface properties (functional groups, carbonization degree) was established. Pyrolysis temperature controlled the content,


