4.4 Organic waste utilization as phytostimulants
Organic phytostimulants or plant biostimulants are complex and heterogeneous organic substances that improve plant productivity and quality by activating different physiological and molecular processes in crop plants [157, 158]. Organic phytostimulants contain materials or microorganisms that may act as fertilizers and increase plant nutrition processes independently of the product’s nutritional level and may also enhance one or more of the following traits of the plant and/ or the rhizosphere of the plant, such as (i) nutrient usage efficiency, (ii) biotic stress tolerance/resistance, (iii) quality traits, or (iv) availability of restricted nutrients in the soil or rhizosphere [157, 159]. Phytostimulants can originate from diverse raw materials with different compositions that can significantly reduce the input of chemical fertilizers and agrochemicals in soils and hence, promote the environmental sustainability of agriculture. Their use as organic phytostimulants provides an interesting possibility for reducing the environmental stresses on agricultural systems [157]. These organic phytostimulants have been grouped into different families: seaweed extract, protein hydrolysates, humic and fulvic acid, chitosan, beneficial microorganisms and other complex phytostimulants [160–162]. However, the mechanisms of phytostimulants action are still being investigated. The main organic phytostimulants are summarized in this section.
4.4.1 Seaweeds extract
Seaweeds extract (SWE) or other plant extracts are either applied directly on soils or as foliar treatment in the form of purified products (such as the products of polysaccharides such laminarin, alginates, and carrageenans) [163, 164]. Microand macro-nutrients, N-containing chemicals (betaines), sterols, and phytohormones including auxins, cytokinins, and gibberellins are other components in seaweed extracts that support plant development [165]. The polysaccharides like laminarin, alginates, carrageenans, or their products may result in gel formation to enhance water retention. The polyanionic compounds in SWE helps in the fixation and exchange of certain cations and HMs for soil remediation. SWE may also involve some antioxidants or endogenous stress responsive genes regulators. Raja and Vidya [162] reported that seaweeds extracts contain bioactive compounds that activate several signaling pathways and defense-related genes/ enzymes. Seaweed extracts and their components induce tolerance, mitigate stresses, can enhance nutrient efficiency and boost growth under stress conditions in agriculture.
4.4.2 Protein hydrolysates
Protein hydrolysates (PHs) consist of peptides or amino acids, which are derived from the enzymatic or chemical hydrolysis of proteins, and are derived either from plants or animals. PHs are recognized as biostimulants for sustainable crop production systems. PHs have been demonstrated to modulate N uptake and assimilation, stimulate shoot and root biomass resulting in enhanced plant growth and yield of various crops [166]. Protein hydrolysates obtained from animals acted as biostimulant for transplanted strawberry plants subjected to cold stress [167]. Some of the PHs may protect plants against HMs, improve soil microbial biomass and activities leading to increases in micronutrient mobility and soil fertility, which might be indirectly enhancing nutrient uptake and plant biomass [168]. Compounds such as glycine, betaine and proline derived from PHs confer antioxidant activity and contribute to the mitigation of various abiotic stresses [169]. However, the effectiveness of different PHs is dependent on various factors, including source, production process and plant species.
4.4.3 Chitosan
Chitosan is a biodegradable and biocompatible polysaccharide obtained from partial chemical or enzymatic deacetylation of chitin. It is the second most common natural polysaccharide after cellulose. Due to its natural origin and multidirectional bioactivity of this polycationic polymer, chitosan is being used as a biostimulant, a plant protection product, an elicitor, or an agent to increase the storage stability of plant raw materials [170]. Chitosan has antifungal, antibacterial, antiviral, and antioxidant activity, and is involved in induction of plant defense mechanisms and regulation of metabolic processes. The purified preparations obtained from brown algae such as laminarin and chitosan-based nanomaterials


