4.4.4 Complex biostimulants
Complex biostimulants include non-pathogenic bacteria, mycorrhizal fungi and other beneficial microorganisms, which inhabit the soil, rhizosphere, as endophytes or in phyllosphere [174–176]. These microbes improve soil fertility, and help the plants by improving their nutrient uptake, by mitigating abiotic stresses and may prevent invasion of pathogens and pests [160, 177–179]. Many microbial biostimulants increase crop yield and quality under pot house and field conditions [180–182]. Some antagonistic rhizospheric bacteria such as Pseudomonas and Bacillus, and fungal endophytes like Trichoderma spp., Sebacinales, Phanerocheate etc. are being used as biocontrol agents and as biopesticides [147, 183, 184]. The symbiotic association of arbuscular mycorrhizal fungi (AMF) with crop plants is widely recommended to promote sustainable agriculture [185, 186]. AMF treatment helps in absorption of macro- and micro-nutrients by plants, in maintaining water balance and mitigating biotic and abiotic stresses [187, 188]. The tripartite associations between mycorrhizal fungi, plant and rhizobacteria along with application of compost have been reported to improve soil quality and increase plant health [189, 190]. Inoculation with mutualistic endosymbionts like Rhizobium and plant growth promoting rhizobacteria (PGPR) is mostly favoured as biostimulants [191, 192]. Single or dual microbial inoculation was found as an efficient biostimulant in legumes with multifunctional growth promoting traits [193]. For instance, coinoculation of chickpea with Mesorhizobium sp. Cicer strain Ca181 and Pseudomonas strains MRS23 and CRP55b, increased the nodule formation by 68.2–115.4% on chickpea at 80 and 100 days after planting under sterile conditions in comparison to single inoculation of Mesorhizobium strain [194]. Shoot dry weight ratios of coinoculated treatments at different stages of plant growth varied from 1.18 to 1.35 times that of Mesorhizobium-inoculated and 3.25 to 4.06 times those of uninoculated plants. In similar studies, Sindhu et al. [195] reported that coinoculation of Bradyrhizobium strain S24 with Bacillus strains in green gram (Vigna radiata) caused 1.28 to 3.55-fold enhancement in shoot dry mass at 40 days of plant growth. Coinoculation of Bradyrhizobium strain with majority of Bacillus strains promoted the nodule number on green gram roots and also increased expression of nitrogenase activity under pot house conditions. Under salt stress conditions, separate inoculations of Rhizobium sp. LSMR-32 and Enterococcus mundtii LSMRS-3 improved sprouting of seeds, seed harvest, crop’s physical characters like height and biomass, chlorophyll concentration, and nutrient assimilation as compared to uninoculated crops [196]. Inoculation with both these strains also enhanced the quantity of leghemoglobin, nodule number, and nodule biomass along with phosphatase and dehydrogenase enzyme levels of soil. The link between the microbiome and plant health has recently been reviewed [197] and microbiome applications showed significant impacts on plant development and sustainable crop production.
4.5 Production of biofuels and chemicals from lignocellulosic biomass
Rapid population expansion and advancement in industrialization and modernization has led to a hike in the demand of energy in recent years [198]. This has resulted in the depletion of conventional energy resources such as oil, natural gas and coal. On the other hand, the use of fossil fuels has caused drastic pollution of air and water resources, along with increase in global temperature and ozone layer depletion [2, 199, 200]. Recently, utilization of agricultural waste for the production of energy has emerged as innovative technology for replacement of fossil fuels in the production of sustainable energy [198, 201, 202]. Crop residues primarily consist of lignocellulosic biomass, which is widely available as a low-cost renewable feedstock. Nearly half the quantity of rice-husk produced in India is used as animal-fodder and the other half portion is discarded in the environment or burnt by the poor farmers. Therefore, appropriate technology may be developed for conversion of rice-husk into useful products after value addition including electricity and biofuel. Recently, many studies


