 value-added products such as biocoal, biofertilizers, bio-bricks, bio-plastics, paper, biofuels, bioelectricity, chemicals, organic acids and industrial enzymes are currently being produced from lignocellulosic waste, which has large impact on the economy [8, 202, 204].
5 Influence of organic amendments on different biological processes in soil
For providing sufficient food to exponentially increasing human population, intensive agriculture practices are followed by farmers worldwide, which generates significant amounts of organic agricultural waste [8]. For processing of organic waste, various biological technologies such as composting, anaerobic digestion, vermicomposting and biochar production are preferentially employed [89, 110, 113]. These sustainable and eco-friendly technologies ensure food security along with mitigation of pollution problems. For instance, the amendment of compost, vermicompost and biochar to the soil has been found to increase soil fertility, stimulation of beneficial microbial community, mitigation of HM stress, suppression of soilborne pathogens and detoxification of pesticides leading to increased plant growth and crop harvest along with less dependence on chemical fertilizers [28, 32, 147, 206] (Fig. 3). Massaccesi et al. [62] reported that the biomass of lettuce (Lactuca sativa L.) plants was increased from 2- to 2.5-fold in all compost and biochar amended soils in comparison to the control after 45 days of cultivation.
5.1 Increase in nutrient availability in soil
Optimal environment, sufficient moisture, and the right quantity of nutrients are essential for the development of crop plants. But, the soil’s potential to deliver nutrients is diminishing with time due to soil erosion, land degradation and intensive crop production systems [57, 207]. For enhancing crop productivity, farmers use excessive amount of inorganic fertilizers, which have deleterious effects on soil health leading to unsustainable yields [208], and also cause environmental pollution leading to public health hazards [6]. Continuous use of chemical fertilizers in the field also affects the proliferation of beneficial microbial community in the soil [209]. In order to increase crop yield, it is necessary to upgrade the nutrient distribution system in a sustainable way to provide adequate food to exponentially rising human population [33, 210, 211]. The effect of different types of organic amendments (OAs) (i.e., unamended control, conventional farmyard manure (FYM), kraal manure, improved FYM, compost, vermicompost, urban waste, taroka, poultry droppings, pig manure) and earthworm Pontoscolex corethrurus (Rhinodrilidae) inoculation was assessed on the growth and nutrition of rice [135]. The poultry droppings had the best fertilizer performance, followed by improved farmyard manure and pig manure in treatments without earthworms after eight weeks of growth. Recently, application of processed products obtained from organic waste materials during composting, vermicomposting, biochar and phytostimulants has emerged as novel agri-technologies for preparing nutrient rich fertilizers [32, 35, 106, 212].


