5.1.1 Enhancement of nutrient availability by composting
Addition of compost in the farm soil increases nutrient levels and improves soil structure [9]. It also improves soil fertility, porosity and soil microbial biomass [213]. A good quality compost is an excellent source of three macronutrients for the plant i.e., nitrogen, phosphorous and potassium (NPK). Although, as compared to inorganic fertilizers, their concentrations in compost is low, but their regular application may fulfill the plants nutrients need, and hence, reduce the application of chemical fertilizers. Also, addition of compost in soils can benefit by altering the soil’s heavy metal bioavailability and can mitigate their toxic effects on animals and plants due to different processes such as adsorption, precipitation and redox reactions. The leachates obtained from the composting process (used as raw or diluted form) were found to serve as substrate for utilization by microbes involved in composting process in shake-flask fermentation assays. For example, in comparison to raw leachate, Bacillus sp. thrived better in diluted leachate [214]. Amendments of leachates with yeast and whey permeate improved the growth of Bacillus sp. and also the sporulation rate. Co-substrates produced the greatest sporulation rate, causing over 89% of vegetative cells to produce spores. Results indicated that leachate valorization approach will reduce the volume of leachate waste for treatment and will also help the circular economy produce biological amendments at a reasonable price. In another study, improvement in biological phosphorus transformation and nutrient content in composting products was observed by inoculation of kitchen waste with phosphorus-solubilizing bacteria (PSB) [215]. Treatment with PSB considerably improved enzyme activity and enhanced the levels of bacterial diversity, total P, Olsen P, citric acid P, and organic matter (OM) decomposition. In a recent study, Priya et al. [216] explored the application of soil amendments compost (CMP) and gypsum, and s