5.1.2 Contribution of vermicomposting towards enhancement of nutrient availability
Soil amendment with vermicompost increased the amount of stored carbon under rice-rice cropping pattern [217] and promoted root growth as well as ginsenoside content of Panax ginseng [218]. Supplementation of vermicompost with recommended dose of inorganic fertilizers was found to enhance N, P and K content in soil in comparison to using only inorganic fertilizers. Sahariah et al. [217] studied soil physical parametres during vermicomposting process and it was observed that addition of vermicompost in the soil improved its water holding capability and decreased bulk density, in comparison to the addition of NPK alone or NPK with farmyard manure. Addition of vermicompost also enhanced microbial biomass as well as their enzyme activities (dehydrogenase, urease, phosphatase and aryl sulfatase) in soil [219]. Increased microbial biomass and dehydrogenase activity was positively correlated with increased ammonical N, nitrate N and orthophosphates. These factors increased nutrient cycling rates in soil and promoted synthesis of plant growth regulating substances.
5.1.3 Enhancement of nutrient availability by biochar application
Biochar amendment in soil results in increased nutrient cycling and improvement in soil fertility, water retention capacity and enhanced microbial activities and also influenced soil characteristics [42, 146, 220, 221]. Different biotic and environmental stresses in soil were found to affect the mineralization rate of biochar [222]. Biochar addition to soil has also been reported to reduce greenhouse gas emissions from soils [148, 223]. The biological, chemical, and physical characteristics of soil can all be considerably enhanced by biochar [221], including soil pH, nutrient availability, cation-exchange capacity (chemical properties); bulk density, porosity and water retention capacity (physical properties); and improvement in microbial population, variety and activity of soil microbes (biological properties). The results of biochar application on soil depends on different variables such as type of feedstock used, conditions at the time of biochar production, the type of soil to which biochar is applied, together with the quantity of biochar used [42, 222]. Amendment of biochar resulted in improved soil fertility and sequestration of carbon and also increased crop yield, alleviated plant stresses, immobilized HMs and reduced their uptake by plants in soil contaminated with organic pollutants [40, 224]. Application of both compost and biochar to soil further enhances its agronomic values as well as results in minimum nutrient loss, which can potentially replace nitrogen fertilizers [225]. Addition of biochar to composts improved its properties and biochar amendment improved aeration in compost because of low density and high porosity of biochar, thereby increasing the humification process and overall microbial activities [226]. Also, because of high sorption ability of biochar, it may reduce excess N loss from compost. Biochar can also immobilize organic pollutants and HMs during composting and can lower emissions of greenhouse gases [224, 227]. Bouhia et al. [228] reported that addition of biochar singly or in biochar-compost combination (BCC) in Zea mays grown in pots filled with alkaline soil increased soil TOC, EC, and pH under greenhouse conditions. Furthermore, adding biochar and compost increased the levels of macro- and micronutrients compared to those under single application of biochar. The soil fertility improved significantly with regard to available P and K, N, and micronutrients. Although, single application of biochar had a negative impact on mycorrhizal symbiosis but single application of compost gave the best results in terms of plant growth and soil fertility improvement. Xie et al. [189] carried out four treatments in ginger field soil i.e., control (no compost or PGPR; CK), compost (TC), compost + Bacillus subtilis (TC-BS), and compost + Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SQR9 (TC-BA). According to the findings, TC considerably enhanced soil organic matter content by 17.34%, alkali hydrolysable nitrogen by 21.66%, available P by 19.56%, and accessible K by 37.20% when compared to the control (CK). Neutral phosphatase, sucrase, and urease activity in the soil all rose by 55.89, 35.59, and 57.21%, respectively. According to research by Ali et al. [229], initial reductive soil disinfestation (RSD) treatment, biochar amendment (RSD-BC), and collective applications of microbes (such as Trichoderma and Bacillus spp.) and biochar (RSD-SQR-T37-BC) increased soil pH, soil organic carbon, and ammonium in the treated soils while lowering nitrate concentration. Li et al. [109] provided an overview of biochar application for organic waste co-composting and its biochemical transformation mechanism. As an amendment in composting, biochar worked in the adsorption of nutrients, the retention of oxygen and water, and the promotion of electron transfer. Biochar addition caused changes in community structure, and mediated metabolic activities involved organic matter degradation. The participation of biochar enriched the α-diversity of microbial communities at all stages of composting,


