5.1.4 Enhancement of nutrient availability and soil fertility by biogas slurry
The biogas slurry discharged from the common anaerobic digesters (biogas plants) is used as organic manure to improve nutrient availability and soil fertility. The cattle dung after passing through the gas plant becomes rich manure and it contains 2% nitrogen as against 0.75% in ordinary compost manure. The domestic biogas digester slurry could also be used in the production of vermicompost [232]. The residual manure of biogas plant helps to meet the fertilizer requirements of farmers in a more economical and efficient manner. Thus, application of biogas slurry offers an alternative to chemical fertilizers and it offers a promising approach to a biogas-linked agroecosystem that restores sustainable coordination between cropping and animal husbandry under an intensive agriculture production system. It helps in


