5.2 Stimulation of beneficial microbes in soil
Specific microorganisms including bacteria, fungi, algae, actinobacteria, archaea, protozoa, and nematodes inhabit the soil and rhizosphere, and influence the development and growth of crop plants [33, 182, 234, 235]. The interactions of surrounding microbes with plants are controlled by exchange of signalling molecules in their environment [236, 237]. The narrow area in close contact with the surface of root, termed rhizosphere, is most affected area by microbial activities [238]. In the rhizosphere, plant roots secrete a wide spectrum of metabolites in root exudates ranging from organic acids, sugars, alcohols, amino acids, nucleotides, and secondary metabolites [239, 240]. These exuded plant metabolites play important role in fostering beneficial interactions with the surrounding microbial community in the plants’ rhizosphere and modulate the dynamics of plant–microbe interactions in fulfilling plant niche-based requirements [241, 242]. It has been estimated that in general bulk soil contains 107 to 109 bacterial cells gram−1 and there is almost 10 to 100fold more bacteria in the rhizosphere [243, 244]. Approximatly, 30,000 prokaryotic species inhabiting the soil have been found to impact the development and productivity of plants [240, 245]. The configuration of rhizosphere microbiota prominently affects the communications between plants and soil, thus helping plants in adapting towards adverse environmental conditions including biotic and abiotic stresses [178, 246, 247]. The soil amended with low concentrations (at 0.01% and 0.1%) of pyroligneous acid (PA; obtained from pyrolysis of lignocellulosic raw material) increased microbial diversity and dehydrogenase activity in comparison to the control treatment and high concentrations (1% and 5%) of PA amendment to soil [248]. Interestingly, population of the plant growth promoting bacteria (PGPB) i.e., Azospirillum, Acetobacter, Beijerinckia, Bradyrhizobium, Herbaspiriluum, Mesorhizobium, Nitrosomonas, Pseudomonas and, Rhizobium was stimulated at lower concentrations of PA. The application of phosphorus fertilizer enriched with microorganisms including Paenibacillus polymyxa strain CHT114AB, B. amyloliquefaciens strain AF75BB and Bacillus sp. strain CZP4/4 was found to improve enzymatic activity and the genetic and functional diversity of the soil microbial communities [249]. The addition of compost and inoculation of beneficial microbes preferentially stimulated the specific microflora in the soil. For instance, ginger field soil was treated with “compost (TC), compost + Bacillus subtilis (TC-BS), and compost + Bacillus amyloliquefaciens SQR9 (TC-BA)” [189]. TC-BS treatment significantly upgraded soil bacterial variety than control treatment i.e., no compost or PGPR “(CK) and TC, and relative abundance of beneficial Proteobacteria, Acidobacteria, Chloroflexi, and Bacillus” microorganisms dominated. This study demonstrated that B. subtilis inoculation increased the variety of bacteria, changed the bacterial population framework, boosted advantageous microorganisms, and encouraged a flourishing rhizosphere in soil from a ginger field treated with tomato compost. In subsequent studies, five fertilization treatments i.e., “chemical fertilizer (CF) and chemical fertilizer combined with biochar (CFBC), manure (M), MBC (manure combined with biochar and FMBC)” decreased the ammonia-oxidizing bacteria (AOB) by 33.1–81.9% [105]. The redundancy analysis demonstrated that “pH, available P, carbon to nitrogen (C/N) ratio, soil moisture content, nitrate nitrogen, and ammonium nitrogen” present in the rhizosphere influence the population structure of ammonia-oxidizing archaea and ammonia-oxidizing bacteria.


