5.3 Bioremediation of heavy metals
Heavy metals such as lead, arsenic, mercury, copper, nickel, cadmium and zinc are being accumulated in soils due to various natural (volcanic eruptions and forest fires) as well as anthropogenic activities like application of chemical fertilizers, sewage sludge disposal, release of industrial waste, power stations emissions, fuel combustion by automobiles etc. (Fig. 4). Although, some of these metals are essential for plant growth in trace amounts, but their excess concentrations can poison the soil and negatively affect plant growth, physiology, metabolism and crop production. In soil, these HMs are non-biodegradable [253]. The physical and chemical treatment of HM remediation in soils is a laborious, expensive and non-sustainable process. Therefore, scientists are engaged in developing technology to sustainably remediate HMs from agronomic soils by using different metal tolerant microorganisms as bioremediation agents [18, 21]. Furthermore, mitigation of heavy metal pollution in soils through addition of compost, vermicompost and biochar has gained considerable attention because of its sustainability, low cost and health benefits [134, 254].
