Under the abiotic stress caused due to accumulation of HMs, growth of soil microbiota and their functioning in different ecological processes is negatively affected. Bioremediation involves the use of microorganisms to clean the polluted environment by detoxifing different types of pollutants [21, 255, 256]. These soil microbes employ a variety of mechanisms to survive under conditions of metal toxicity. They can store, alter, modify or detoxify HMs by binding, immobilizing, oxidizing and volatilizing them [257, 258]. The basic methods of bioremediation of soil pollutants by microorganisms include biosorption, immobilization, bioaugmentation and biotransformation (Table 1). Generally, two types of bioremediation i.e., in situ and ex situ methods have been adopted by metal tolerant microbes for successful remediation of HMs from soil. During in situ method, genetic engineering techniques are used, while in the ex situ method, the contaminated soil is removed from its original site, moisturized and amended with nutrients [259] Some common examples of metal-tolerant microorganisms include Bacillus, Pseudomonas aeruginosa (Zn and Cu), Citrobacter spp. and Chlorella vulgaris (Cd, Pb, U, Cu, Au, Ni, Hg), Aspergillus niger (Cd, U, Zn), and Rhizopus arrhizus (Cd, Ca, Hg, Pb, Ag) [260]. Application of compost and biochar in soil also contributed in mitigation of HMs stress using physico-chemical approach in the HM polluted soil [104]. The stoichiometric interactions between different microbial enzymes such as N-acetylglucosaminidase, β-glucosidase, leucine aminopeptidase and acid phosphatase were examined after application of heavy metals. Application of biochar to HM-polluted soils enhanced the activity of acid phosphatase and inhibited activities of other enzymes. In addition, application of biochar-compost strengthened all these enzymes activities, which were inhibited due to HMs. However, the stoichiometric ratio of C/P, C/N and N/P were changed due to application of the biochar and compost. Along with alleviating the phosphorous-limitation in soils under copper stress, biochar also alleviated the carbon-limitation in soils under Cd, As and Zn stress. Gao et al. [261] reviewed the recent advances in bioaugmentation technology for efficiently removing poisonous contaminants from organic waste contaminated soils by using microbes or their enzymes and bioremediation additives. A thorough overview of several bioaugmentation techniques, including bacteria, fungi, their enzymes, and some other bioremediation additives, was provided. The longterm stability of wide-range bioremediation carriers, microbial/enzyme agents targeting coupled pollutants, desorption of ecologically beneficial additives, and minute molecular biological stimulants were considered, while evaluating the future of bioaugmentation. Li et al. [262] used vinegar-residue biochar for remediation of heavy metal cadmium from contaminated water and soil. Rwiza et al. [263] pyrolyzed lignocellulosic materials i.e., rice husks and maize hulls, which were burned at 500 °C to create biochar, and were subsequently activated with KOH and ZnCl2 as activating agents. Compared to the raw hulls
