5.4 Biocontrol ofphytopathogens
Plant pathogens coupled with drastic climate changes are the major constraints for improving crop productivity and providing healthy food for ever-increasing human population. Various pathogenic microorganisms cause different diseases on major crops affecting up to 20–40% annual losses in crop yields globally, which seriously jeopardize food security and the quality of agricultural production [267, 268]. Farmers use excessive amounts of toxic pesticides for control of phytopathogens and insects to minimize crop losses. However, injudicious application of these pesticides causes environmental pollution, development of resistance in pathogens, disturbs ecological biodiversity and even causes public health hazards [5]. Hence, a sustainable and economical option is needed for controlling plant diseases and improving crop yield. Recently, biological control of pathogens and pests by the use of antagonistic microorganisms (biocontrol agents), their extracted bioactive metabolites or organic amendments has emerged as an environment friendly and cost effective technology for controlling/killing the pathogens and suppression of plant diseases [269, 270]. These biological methods of controlling phytopathogens will decrease the excessive use of agro-chemicals in crop fields and will reduce the pollution as well as other health related problems [271–273]. In a stride to assess the significance of organic amendments on biological control of phytopathogens, different amendments either singly or in combination with biocontrol agents were tested for suppression of the plant diseases. For instance, pyroligneous acid (PA) or wood vinegar (an acidic reddish-brown aqueous liquid), possessing antifungal, termiticidal and antimicrobial activities, has been produced through the pyrolysis of lignocellulosic raw materials [274, 275]. In another experiment, vermicompost application was found to reduce the number of parasitic nematodes and encouraged the fungivorous and bacterivorous nematodes in soil [131]. In another study, three organic soil amendments viz., cassava peel (Manihot esculenta, Cranz), sawdust (Gmelina arborea, Roxb) and leaves of Spanish cedar (Cedrela odorata L.) were evaluated on the stalk rot disease of maize (Zea mays L.) caused by Fusarium verticillioides [276]. Interestingly, maize plants subjected to cassava peels combined with C. odorata treatment showed significant reduction in disease index and disease severity due to favourable competition with pathogenic fungi F. verticillioides. All these soil treatments also showed significant growth promoting effects on plant height, leaves number, leaves surface area and stems girths as compared to control plants. Ali et al. [229] carried out initial reductive soil disinfestation (RSD) and applications of Bacillus (RSD-SQR), Trichoderma (RSD-T37), and biochar (RSD-BC) reduced the incidence of wilt by 26.8, 37.5, and 32.5%, respectively in comparison to nonRSD treatments. Greater suppressiveness of Fusarium wilt and F. oxysporum was observed in treatments with RSD-SQRT37-BC and RSD-T37. In similar studies, use of integrated pest management (IPM) approaches statistically decreased the Fusarium wilt severity of lettuce from 50 to 70% in comparison to untreated controls, in both fields under two consecutive years [277]. The following treatments were used: (i) compost enriched with Trichoderma, (ii) combination of “Trichoderma gamsii + T. asperellum, Bacillus amyloliquefaciens and potassium posphite”, and (iii) combination of “T. polysporum + T. arviridus”. An increased crop yield was also observed in comparison to untreated controls. High-throughput sequencing of bacterial and eukaryotic rRNA gene markers of rhizosphere microbiota was performed and it showed Proteobacteria, Firmicutes and Actinobacteria as predominant phyla for bacteria and Ascomycota for fungi.


