5.5 Remediation of pesticides and organic pollutants
For the management of pathogenic fungii, weeds, and insect pests in crop fields, a variety of pesticides, including fungicides, herbicides, and insecticides, are often utilized. Nevertheless, fewer than 1% of the insecticides used in field applications actually reach the pests [279]. The residual pesticides harm the environment and human health because of their toxicity and environmental pollution [5]. In addition, various organic contaminants in agricultural soils including detergents, dyes, lipids, phenols, oil and surfactants are continually being added in the soil through various processes. Thus, the occurrence of pesticides and organic pollutants in soil is a major environmental concern. Use of single or groups of bacteria, microbial-algae consortia, or organic amendments (compost or biochar) has been recommended as a sustainable approach to decontaminate or detoxify the pesticides and organic pollutants from the environment [46, 75]. Ling et al. [280] reported that amendment of soil with Biochar500 or Biochar600 (pyrolyzed at 500 °C and 600 °C, respectively) induces a shift in microbial community structure towards dominance of Gemmatimonadetes, Bacteroidia, Alphaproteobacteria and Gammaproteobacteria classes and promotes faster mineralization of SOC, thus offsetting C sequestration effects. The majority of soil derived CO2 efflux in biochar amended soils originated from recalcitrant components that were mineralized by the persistent organic matter decomposers. The application of biochar and wood vinegar together prevented glyphosate pesticides’ hazardous leaking into the aquatic environment [281]. In another study, humic acids exhibited a potent capacity to sorb cationic and hydrophobic organic pollutants as it was shown that the functional groups of humic acid, such as quinones, may establish covalent connections with aromatic amines or similar chemical molecules [282]. According to certain reports, lignin-modifying enzymes (LMEs) such laccase and peroxidase 