6 Plant growth stimulation and increases in crop yield
Amendments of soil with compost, vermicompost and biochar have been established as a effective method for increasing crop yields, while lowering the need for chemical fertilizers, leading to sustainable agriculture [37, 57, 63]. In addition, 10–40% photosynthetically fixed carbon is deposited within the rhizosphere primarily as root biomass, exudates, and microbial biomass as soil SOM [129, 239]. Moreover, combined application of organic amendments alongwith single or consortium of beneficial microbes has been found to enhance biomass and yield of different crops under field and greenhouse conditions [37, 182, 286] (Table 2). The synergistic effects of organic amendments with beneficial microbes increase the accessibility of nutrients including N, P, K, Zn and S, mitigate abiotic stresses and suppress plant diseases. Thus, supply of carbon and energy resources from decomposed plant residues in the form of SOM alongwith root exudates help in proliferation of beneficial microbial community. Subsequent expression of plant growth promoting activities of beneficial microbes leads to stimulation of plant growth and increases in crop yield, contributing to circular sustainable agriculture systems. But in some instances, some limitations have been reported, which restrict crop development in a variety of agricultural settings. For example, the survival, viability and functionality of inoculated microbes under field conditions are often different under laboratory and greenhouse environments due to competition with indigenous microbes and it adversely impact the growth of crop plants [287]. Therefore, organic compounds, cell protectants, and nanoparticles are frequently added to beneficial biofertilizer strains to boost their survival and effectiveness for improving crop yield [33]. The effectiveness of various organic amendments including compost, vermicompost and biochar on yield of different crop varied depending on soil management, SOM content, rate of application and frequency of chemical fertilizers in integrated nutrient management (INM) system as well as on the addition of other soil amendments. For instance, addition of municipal waste compost at a dose of 80 tonnes ha−1 annually for 5 years, enhanced the wheat yield by 246% in comparison to untreated control in a field study [288]. In similar studies, Erhart et al. [289] reported 10% increase in the yields of cereals and potato crop by application of of biowaste compost (23 tonnes ha−1 year−1) compared to unfertilized control treeatment. Using INM approach, Diacono and Montemurro [290] showed that application of organic amendments along with mineral fertilizers increased the plant growth and crop yield compared to application of soil amendment alone. Recently, the development of ecological circular agriculture system has highly encouraged the use of biogas slurry as an important kind of fertilizer owing to its high nutrient components, environmentally friendly and lower costs than chemical fertilizers. Biogas slurry can be added directly to the crops or it may be used along with other organic amendments and synthetic stimulants. For example, Yu et al. [118] observed that amendment of biogas slurry in soil improved the soil texture and enhanced the crop yield and fruit qualities of tomato. Similar studies carried out by Pathak et al. [291] showed that seeds soaked in biogas slurry improved germination rates and increased seedling growth than untreated seeds. Zhao et al. [292] sprayed biogas slurry on Capsicum plants in the field and it increased crop yields and prevented the diseases. Addition of animal urine along with biogas slurry was found to increase its N content, resulting in increased C:N ratio for easy nutrient accessibility to plants and soil microflora. On the other hand, long-term application of untreated biogas slurry in crop lands has been found to adversely affect the crop yield, resulting in losses of crop yield and quality [293, 294]. The continuous application of biogas slurry has also been reported to increase the accumulation of heavy metals or genes for antibiotic resistance in soil [295, 296]. It may also cause leaching or movement of extra nutrients into


