groundwater or surface water. Therefore, pre-treatment of biogas slurry has been suggested as safe strategy before its application in farmlands. Schmidt et al. [297] reported that amendments to a vineyard soil with biochar alone (8 t ha−1), aerobic compost (55t ha−1) and biochar-compost (8t ha−1 + 55t ha−1) induced small and non-significant effects on fruit quality, health of plants, nutrient absorption, and development of plants over the three years. Vine growing in less fertile, alkaline and temperate soil conditions did not immediately benefit economically from applying more biochar to the top soil. Ding et al. [41] demonstrated that typically biochar has a large surface area with many functional groups, a high cation exchange capacity, and great stability. Therefore, biochar application to soil was suggested to play critical role in enhancing the fertilizer use efficiency, nutrient availability and exchange of nutrients. Thus, biochar application increases the crop yield. However, before it is feasible to precisely evaluate the sustainability of this strategy to trap carbon and restore soil function, further research on the long-term effects of adding biochar on soil characteristics is still needed. The co-application of wood vinegar and biochar was found to enhance the fertility of soil and also promoted plant development by enhancing seed sprouting and seedling development [298]. Yu et al. [299] presented an overview about problem soils (that inhibit or prevent plant growth) and application of biochars as useful soil amendment materials. The interactions of biochar with soil particles, soil microflora and roots of plants were discussed in relation to remediation of pollutants from contaminated soil and improvement in plant growth. Authors suggested that adding biochar to contaminated soils is a practical strategy to improve their quality and boost crop productivity. Bouhia et al. [228] showed that addition of biochar to compost increased the levels of macro- and micro-nutrients compared to those under single application of biochar. The soil fertility improved significantly with regard to available P and K, N, and micronutrients with the addition of biochar singly or in biochar-compost combination in Zea mays grown in pots. Overall, single application of compost gave the best results in terms of plant growth and soil fertility improvement. In another study, Ebrahimi et al. [300] reported that soil amendment with vermicompost and pistachio biochar and 100% plant water requirement (PWR) showed the best plant growth of eggplant (Solanum melongena L.). Application of both pistachio biochar and vermicompost at 100% PWR resulted in the highest leaf concentrations of N, P, K, Fe and Mn. The total plant yield was found highest under combined application of vermicompost and pistachio biochar at 100% PWR. Salvador et al. [187] reported that organic compost containing Bacillus subtilis resulted in a 50% increase in root dry weight for soybean plants in comparison to soils without any amendment, whereas plants grown in Rhizophagus irregularis soil showed higher nodule biomass (∼30%), symbiotic efficiency (∼50%), chlorophyll content (∼20%) and nitrogen accumulation (∼45%). In a stride to establish the role of organic amendments on yield attributes and to reduce drought stress-induced damages in wheat (Triticum aestivum), Anee et al. [81] added different OAs consisting of compost, vermicompost and poultry manure @ 0.09 kg m−2 soil, biochar @ 2.5% w/w soil and chitosan @ 1% w/w soil. Addition of vermicompost enhanced soil plant analysis and development (SPAD) value by 18%, number of spikelets spike−1 by 20%, number of grains spike−1 by 17%, whereas poultry manure amendment increased plant height by 16% under drought condition compared to control plant. Vermicompost also showed the capability to reduce the level of lipid peroxidation and raised the proline content by drought condition. Drought stress-induced increment of catalase, ascorbate peroxidase and glutathione reductase activities were also efficiently modulated by the organic amendment application. Bornø et al. [301] used wood biochar or straw treatments to maize seedlings that were subjected to various irrigation schedules. In the early drought and recovery phases, biochar additions had a beneficial impact on leaf water potential and photosynthetic metrics, but had no impact on plant biomass. Additionally, biochar impacted the pH and ammonium content of the soil, and also boosted the C-substrate utilization in the biochar treatments, irrespective of watering. Drought particularly had an additive effect on the level of succinic acid in the wood biochar treatment, whereas biochar specifically raised the content of organic acids. In similar studies, Ghorbani et al. [302] examined wheat’s ability to utilize nitrogen in connection to azocompost (A), biochar (B) combined with urea (U), and leftovers from legumes (L). The treatments included: “Control (no biochar, no fertilizer), U (46 kg ha−1), A [5 tonnes (t) ha−1], L (5 t ha−1), B (10 t ha−1), UB, AB, and LB”. With UB and UL treatments, the grain production of wheat was increased by 337% and 312%, respectively. The greatest grain nitrogen yield was achieved by the LB, rising 8.8 times above the control. In comparison to the control, legumes had the highest efficiency in nitrogen use, increasing by 149%. With a value of 91 and 70%, respectively, the use of LB resulted in the best N-harvest index and N-recovery efficiency. Khucharoenphaisan and Sinma [101] observed that addition of rice straw compost (at a dose of 6 tonnes ral−1) showed better growth promotion ability of asparagus (Asparagus officinalis) than peanut straw compost and sugarcane begasse compost. Application of rice straw compost and chemical fertilizer (30 kg ral−1) gave the cumulative number of shoots i.e., 17.2 and 14.0 shoots plant−1, respectively. Sharma et al. [303] produced high-quality stabilized compost from biodegradation of solid waste using decentralized in-vessel composting and its application improved the growth yield of Brassica juncea (mustard), Spinacia oleracea (spinach) and Raphanus sativus (radish).


