The combined application of plant feedstock biochar and vinegar improved the yield of tomato (Solanum lycopersicum L.) plants [304]. In contrast, vinegar application could potentially limit the yield and fruit sugar content of tomato plants treated with animal feedstock biochar. The sugar content was higher for the chicken manure and animal litter biochar, which was attributed to better pH conditions. Rajput et al. [254] reported that individual application of biochar (2.5% of total soil) and/or bacterization with metal-tolerant bacteria (1010CFU kg−1) on barley (Hordeum sativum L) reduced the uptake of zinc in roots by 44% and 20%, and those in the plant’s above-ground portions by 39% and 13%, respectively as compared to contaminated soil treatments with ZnO nanoparticles (ZnO NPs; 2000 mg kg−1 concentration). Separately and in conjunction with bacteria, biochar improved plant height by 53% and 40%, and root length by 48% and 85%, respectively in comparison to the contaminated control. A decrease by 52% and 40% was observed in the root length and plant height in soils spiked with ZnO nanoparticles compared to clean soil treatments. Treatment of contaminated soil with metal-tolerant bacteria decreased the zinc compound mobility by 7%, whereas addition of biochar lowered mobility of zinc mobile components by 33%. By applying both biochar and bacteria together, the mobility of zinc mobile compounds was reduced in soil. Nawaz et al. [305] showed that drought-mediated decrease in photosynthetic pigments and gas exchange characteristics was markedly lower in biochar (BC) and PGPR-treated soybean plants in pots under semi-controlled conditions in comparison with control. Plants treated with BC + Paraburkholderia phytofirmans showed maximum increase in photosynthetic (12–30%) and the enzymatic antioxidant activities (5–20%), whereas no further significant increase was observed in plants treated with combined BC and Bacillus sp. under water deficit conditions (25–30% water holding capacity). Similarly, the combined BC and P. phytofirmans application considerably increased the grain yield (14%) under drought stress conditions. In another study, Behzadi et al. [306] investigated the impact of vermicompost and foliar application of zinc sulfate under conditions of reduced irrigation on the physiological properties of borage (Borago officinalis L.). An increase in vermicompost fertilizer application led to an elevation in relative water content (RWC), with the highest RWC (79.2%) achieved when 10 ton ha−1 of vermicompost was applied. Vermicompost supplementation improved chlorophyll a + b content during the seed-filling stage under conditions of irrigation cut-off. Furthermore, the findings revealed that applying 2 and 4 mg l−1 zinc sulfate reduced malondialdehyde content by 5% and 9%, respectively. The catalase, peroxidase, and superoxide dismutase activities demonstrated an increased response to stress mitigation treatments. However, their activities decreased as vermicompost and zinc sulfate levels increased. The study demonstrated that the highest biomass was obtained when 10 ton ha−1 of vermicompost and 2 mg l−1 of zinc sulfate were applied. Using vermicompost and zinc sulfate, particularly during the seed-filling phase, alleviated the adverse effects of drought stress. Significant increases in the soil pH, organic matter and total N content were reported by higher application rate of biochar (at the dose of 2.5 and 5% biochar level) in soil cultivated with rice [307]. Biochar was found to affect soil microbial community structure/functions, and its higher application rate decreased the soil bacterial diversity but elevated the fungal diversity. However, dominant species taxa were not altered by application of different concentrations of biochar. Thus, biochar application was found to increase rice crop yields by regulating soil microbial taxa composition. However, more research has to be put into figuring out how the different levels of biochar application differentially affect physiological functions of the soil microbes, soil nutrient indexes, crop yields and ecosystem’s health. In another recently conducted experiment, biochar derived from tomato straw (TSB), sweet pepper straw (SPSB), and eggplant straw (ESB) was analyzed for nutrient content, cation exchange capacity and adsorption rates [252]. SPSB showed significantly higher levels of N, P, and K, along with superior adsorption capacity compared to TSB and ESB. Treatment of cucumber with 5% (w/w) SPSB concentration increased plant height by 13.01%, stem thickness by 20.79%, leaf area by 50.26%, and dry weight by 58.56% in comparison to the untreated control under greenhouse conditions. Another recent study highlighted the potential of floral waste as sustainable alternative to traditional synthetic chemicals for use in organic agriculture. For example, El Malahi et al. [308] demonstrated that application of rose distillation waste on seedlings’of Rosa damascena showed a significant increase in terms of leaf area and a maximum apical internode growth of 1.5 mm was observed in seedlings. The incorporation of biostimulant (containing both mycorrhizae and plant growth promoting rhizobacteria) in the rose distillation waste substrate resulted in significant stem height growth of 11.3 cm and a high bud break rate of 73.3%. In similar studies, inoculation of earthworms Pontoscolex corethrurus (Rhinodrilidae) significantly enhanced the fertilizer performance of different types of organic amendments (OAs) [135]. Overall, OAs with low fertilizer performance showed a more pronounced response to earthworms. Additionally, earthworms improved the performance of high-quality OAs likely by enhancing the availability of P and Mg, suggesting synergistic effects of earthworms and OAs of various qualities on rice plant growth in nutrient-poor Ferralsols of Madagascar. The rice grain yield increased with application of biochar at application rates of 1.5% (T1.5), T3.0, and T4.5 as compared to control untreatment (T0) by 116.38, 141.24 and 145.20%, respectively in a saline-alkali paddy field [212].


