7 Future perspectives and scope
Significant increases in the crop yields and quality of food production have been achieved by application of modern agricultural technologies. Some of these technologies utilize chemical fertilizers, pesticides and plant growth regulators for securing sufficient crop yields to feed over seven billions of human beings on the earth. However, extensive and injudicious uses of these agrochemicals have negative impacts on the environment, microbial diversity and human health. Therefore, there is urgent need for sustainable agriculture, which relies on a better understanding of organic waste management as well as beneficial interactions between plants and soil-inhabiting microorganisms. Organic wastes can be processed and utilized in different forms and for various purposes in agriculture. They are mainly utilized for improving soil’s physical and chemical properties, and for nutrient replenishment. In the last few decades, soil contamination with organic and inorganic wastes, pesticides and HMs is also gaining much attention of researchers. Multiple technologies have been devised to manage agricultural waste and to overcome soil pollution [25, 31, 334]. But, all these technologies have some demerits when used individually. Therefore, there is a need to develop integrated techniques or to modify existing methods to get maximum benefits for improving soil fertility and crop production. The modifications or improvements must concentrate to develop sustainable methods, not damaging the environment in the long run, easy to use, ethical, economical and affordable. Most of the organic waste management practices and soil pollution mitigation methods employ different microorganisms at certain stage of agri-technology [28, 110, 113]. The selection of particular microbial strain is the crucial step of any bioconversion technology. Although, microorganisms exhibit remarkable evolutionary capabilities but their natural evolution is relatively slow. In such scenarios, bioengineering of beneficial microorganisms may be helpful to enhance the bioconversion capacity of pollutants [335]. Recent developments in Microbiology, Molecular biology and Biotechnology are providing the driving force towards engineering of better microbes for bioremediation of polluted soil and to obtain improved microorganisms with desired characteristics. Mishra et al. [336] discussed the profiling of microorganisms present in polluted sites using various techniques including culture-based and omics-based approaches. The use of microbial electrochemical technologies was also reviewed for remediation of pollutants. In addition, the effects of various treatment technologies for remediation of pollutants were evaluated and future outlook and challenges were discussed. The use of plant biostimulants and microbiome engineering has recently emerged as environment-friendly technology for improving nutrient use efficiency and mitigating biotic and abiotic stress resulting in plant growth promotion and increases in crop yield [159, 178]. Recently, genetically modified organisms (GMOs) or bioengineered microbes have been developed for faster degradation and removal of soil contaminants such as pesticides, hydrocarbons, and HMs to restore soil health [335]. Applications of GMOs were found to improve the bioremediation process by altering metabolic pathways that results in hyper-secretions of different biomolecules. In order to make the soil productive and healthy, contaminated or polluted soils may be treated with GMOs, PGPR, AMF and other soil additives for effective restoration of soil health [147, 333]. The resource efficiency may further be enhanced by appropriate waste management and valorization processes of agricultural waste biomass into value-added products for execution of sustainable circular bio-economy [337, 338]. Moreover, the synergistic effects of organic amendments with beneficial microbes may become a preferred management option to provide valuable insights for fertilization strategies in nutrient-poor ecosystems, benefiting both plant growth and crop yield components.
8 Conclusions
Production of organic agricultural wastes is unavoidable due to increasing food demand for exponentially growing human population worldwide. But, the accumulation of unprocessed organic waste on soil is causing environmental pollution. Different strategies and technologies used for organic waste management include composting, anaerobic digestion, vermicomposting, biochar production, organic phytostimulants, and bioremediation of pollutants. Application of these processed organic wastes in the farm soil increased nutrient availability, stimulated the population of beneficial microbes, caused remediation of heavy metals, inhibited the growth of phytopathogens, and detoxified or degraded the pesticides and organic pollutants. Furthermore, fertilization of soil or crops with these processed organic materials improved soil fertility and health; and stimulated plant growth resulting to increases in crop yields.


