Thus, appropriate management of organic wastes by using recently developed technologies will not only reduce the application of chemical fertilizers in fields, but will also help the soil to restore its natural physical, chemical and biological properties. Further research on the current and future valorization routes of organic wastes, from detoxification of pollutants and pesticides to the development of promising applications in agriculture, energy, and the environment will have significant implications for low-income countries. However, inconsistent results obtained by the application of biochar, compost and vermicompost in polluted or degraded soils for improving crop productivity and limited understanding of complex plant-microbiome-environmental interactions in soil are still few limitations. More field experiments could help in upscaling the utilization of organic amendment technologies for better understanding of plant–microbe interactions in the field conditions and for harnessing better plant growth and crop yields in sustainable agriculture.
